WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Land-use decisions shape where homes, industry, roads, parks, food, public facilities, and hazards are located.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:17.540
Environmental justice asks whether pollution and health risks are concentrated, whether health-supporting benefits are accessible, and whether affected communities have meaningful power in the decisions.

3
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:35.303
This independent study draws from the California Department of Justice Bureau of Environmental Justice's September twenty twenty-three Best Practices for Implementing Senate Bill one thousand, the DOJ's current SB one thousand page, and current California Government Code section sixty-five thousand three hundred two.

4
00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:38.264
It is not legal advice or a compliance determination.

5
00:00:38.264 --> 00:00:44.380
The DOJ guide explains that discriminatory land-use practices have contributed to unequal pollution burdens.

6
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:54.970
Current general-plan law requires qualifying jurisdictions to identify disadvantaged communities, engage them, and address environmental justice through specified policies and objectives.

7
00:00:54.970 --> 00:01:01.765
Exact duties depend on current law and facts, so the live statute and qualified counsel must guide any real application.

8
00:01:01.765 --> 00:01:03.068
Use a three-part frame.

9
00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:05.899
Distribution asks where burdens and benefits fall.

10
00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:10.600
Procedure asks who receives information, participates, and influences the decision.

11
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:18.641
Recognition asks whether the analysis respects community knowledge and distinct conditions instead of treating every place as interchangeable.

12
00:01:18.641 --> 00:01:20.446
Begin with a land-use pathway.

13
00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:26.344
Identify a decision, such as a designation, buffer, facility investment, or transportation policy.

14
00:01:26.344 --> 00:01:33.144
Trace how it changes exposure, access, housing stability, physical activity, food access, or civic participation.

15
00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:37.115
Then identify which public body controls each part of the pathway.

16
00:01:37.115 --> 00:01:40.245
Do not treat environmental justice as one map layer.

17
00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:46.142
Screening data can guide inquiry, but local evidence, history, and lived conditions are necessary.

18
00:01:46.142 --> 00:01:48.188
State assumptions and uncertainty.

19
00:01:48.188 --> 00:01:49.843
Torrance application segment.

20
00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.410
This segment is separate from the DOJ source.

21
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:58.173
It cites the official City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

22
00:01:58.173 --> 00:02:05.533
That page says the City is updating its Land Use and Safety Elements and will integrate environmental-justice goals and policies.

23
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:11.980
For study, select one hypothetical policy alternative and create a distribution, procedure, and recognition memo.

24
00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.802
Identify possible pollution burdens and health-supporting benefits.

25
00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:20.538
List affected communities without declaring that any area meets a legal definition.

26
00:02:20.538 --> 00:02:24.018
Describe how community evidence could change the alternative.

27
00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:33.147
This exercise does not evaluate Torrance's plan, determine whether a statutory trigger applies, or substitute for current City records and city-attorney advice.

28
00:02:33.147 --> 00:02:42.983
The core lesson is that land use is an environmental-justice issue because public decisions shape exposure, opportunity, and voice over long periods.

29
00:02:42.983 --> 00:02:48.397
The California DOJ source text used here is state-authored public-domain material.

30
00:02:48.397 --> 00:02:54.932
Third-party images, data, local example-policy language, appendices, and linked works are excluded.

31
00:02:54.932 --> 00:02:57.441
No DOJ or City endorsement is implied.
